==Background==
The match marked the first ever UEFA Super Cup between a pair of consecutive UEFA Champions League winners—Chelsea won the [[2011–12 UEFA Champions League]], coincidentally defeating Bayern Munich in the [[2012 UEFA Champions League final|final]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c50fe1ee222-f37c4256bac9-1000--mourinho-and-guardiola-eye-super-cup-glory/|title=Mourinho and Guardiola eye Super Cup glory|date=29 August 2013|website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=30 August 2013 }}</ref>

The match was the first and, as of 2025, only time since [[2004 UEFA Super Cup|2004]] that both finalists competed without the managers who won their respective UEFA trophies in the previous season, as both [[Jupp Heynckes]] and [[Rafael Benítez]] left the clubs after the conclusion of the previous season. As such, this was also the last Super Cup to be won by a new manager replacing the European winning one. The new managers, [[Pep Guardiola]] and [[José Mourinho]], renewed a rivalry they shared in Spain as managers of [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] and [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] respectively.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2013/aug/29/jose-mourinho-chelsea-bayern-munich|title=Super Cup gives Chelsea chance to signal their intent|date=29 August 2013|work=The Guardian|access-date=30 August 2013 }}</ref> Guardiola had twice won the trophy in his management career, with Barcelona in [[2009 UEFA Super Cup|2009]] and [[2011 UEFA Super Cup|2011]]. Mourinho had competed for the trophy only once in [[2003 UEFA Super Cup|2003]] when his [[FC Porto|Porto]] entered as [[2003 UEFA Cup final|UEFA Cup holders]] and were defeated by [[AC Milan|Milan]]. Since then Mourinho had twice won the Champions League but on each occasion left his club immediately afterwards and therefore did not lead them in to the Super Cup.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefasupercup/2013/E/E_2012139_PK.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140513184123/http://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefasupercup/2013/E/E_2012139_PK.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=13 May 2014 |title=Press pack|date=29 August 2013|website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |access-date=30 August 2013 }}</ref> He would repeat this exact feat with [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]], losing the [[2017 UEFA Super Cup]] having entered as [[2017 UEFA Europa League final|UEFA Europa League holders]].

==Ticketing==
The international ticket sales phase for the general public ran from 14 June to 5 July 2013. Tickets were available in three price categories: €130, €90, and €50.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/0250-0c50ff9730c9-8eee195069fb-1000--uefa-super-cup-tickets-go-on-sale/|title=UEFA Super Cup tickets go on sale|website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=14 June 2013 }}</ref> UEFA also launched a charity ticket auction, with all proceeds going to the Centre for Access to Football in Europe.<ref>{{cite web|title=UEFA Super Cup charity ticket auction|url=https://www.facebook.com/EuropaLeague/photos/uefa-has-launched-a-charity-ticket-auction-for-the-uefa-super-cup-between-fc-bay/567354153331815/|date=29 July 2013|website=Facebook: Europa League|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref> The two clubs were allocated tickets where fans could apply.

